    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A cooperative system is one in which there are multiple species
                which benefit from interactions with each other.
                Let us consider bees and flowers, for example.
            </p>

            <p>
                Let <m>x(t)</m> be the population of species 1 (bees) at time
                <m>t</m>, and <m>y(t)</m> be the population of species 2
                (flowers) at time <m>t</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Assume bees decrease proportionally to the current population of
                bees with rate <m>a</m>.
                And assume an encounter between a bee and a flower changes the
                population of bees by rate <m>b</m>.
                Assume flowers also decrease proportionally to the current
                population of flowers with rate <m>c</m>.
                Assume an encounter between a bee and a flower changes the
                population of flowers by rate <m>d</m>.
                (We can assume <m>a, b, c, d</m> are all positive for this
                question, otherwise we may change the nature of the cooperative
                system.)
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In a cooperative system, does an encounter between a bee and
                    a flower increase the populations, decrease the populations,
                    or increase one, but decrease the other?
                    Why?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is your system of differential equations?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What are the equilibrium points (hint there should be two)?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the Jacobian in general, and then evaluate it at the
                    two equilibrium points.
                    Additionally, determine what types of equilibrium points
                    they are (sink, source, saddle, ...).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the parameters <m>{a = 5, b = 3, c = 3, d = 2}</m>, plot
                    both solutions versus time.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using the same parameters as above, create a phase portrait
                    with <m>y</m> vs.
                    <m>x</m>.
                    From your phase portrait identify an initial condition where
                    the solution will have both populations blow up to infinity.
                    Identify a second initial condition where both populations
                    go to zero. Explain/justify your choices.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>